  "summary_en": "European Commission report (COM(2020) 64) accompanying the AI White Paper, assessing gaps in EU product safety and liability frameworks for AI, IoT and robotics. It finds existing law largely technology-neutral but recommends targeted, risk-based adjustments on cybersecurity, autonomy, opacity, software updates, burden of proof and possible strict liability with insurance.",
  "title_clean": "Report on the safety and liability implications of Artificial Intelligence, the Internet of Things and robotics",
  "title_en": "Report on the safety and liability implications of Artificial Intelligence, the Internet of Things and robotics",
  "venue": "European Commission COM(2020) 64 final",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Existing EU product safety law is technology-neutral but has gaps on cybersecurity, mental-health risks, faulty data, opacity and standalone software.",
   "Consider re-assessment of AI products that change through self-learning or software updates, and explicit human oversight requirements.",
   "Consider adjusting the Product Liability Directive, e.g. clarifying software as product and 'putting into circulation'.",
   "Consider easing or reversing burden of proof for victims of AI damage.",
   "Consult on strict liability coupled with mandatory insurance for high-risk AI applications such as autonomous vehicles and delivery drones."
